1 TYPE
    The TYPE command writes one file's entire content to the console as
    text, expanding VFC/Fixed/Variable/Stream record formats into
    readable lines the same way COPY's own text-mode copying does.

    TYPE requires its file specification to match exactly one file --
    unlike DIRECTORY, it does not accept a wildcard that could match more
    than one file.

    TYPE SYS$INPUT types the process's input a line at a time: in a
    command procedure, the data lines after the command, up to the next
    "$" line (see HELP @ and HELP DECK); at the terminal, what is typed,
    up to Ctrl/Z.

    Syntax:

        TYPE [/PAGE] [/HOST] <file-spec>

    Parameter:

        <file-spec>     The file to display, on a mounted ODS-2 volume or
                        on the host.  For a volume file, an omitted
                        version defaults to the highest surviving
                        version, and any part left out is filled in from
                        the current default device/directory (see SET
                        DEFAULT).  A name with a host path ("./notes.txt",
                        quoted to keep its case) is a host file; so is a
                        bare name when the default directory isn't on a
                        mounted volume.  A host file is shown line by
                        line as it is.

    Qualifiers:

        /HOST           Makes <file-spec> a host file name, whatever it
                        looks like.  It may follow the verb or the file:
                        TYPE/HOST "notes.txt" and TYPE "notes.txt"/HOST
                        are the same.

        /PAGE           Display the file a screenful at a time.  After
                        each screen, TYPE prompts "Press RETURN to
                        continue": RETURN shows the next screen, and any
                        text typed before RETURN ends the display.  The
                        screen's size is the terminal's; when that can't
                        be read, the vax.console.lines setting gives its
                        height (default 24 lines).

    Examples:

        TYPE FOO.TXT

        TYPE/PAGE DUA0:[MYDIR]README.TXT;2

        TYPE/PAGE "../docs/notes.txt"

        TYPE "README.md"/HOST

    See also the help for COPY, DIRECTORY, and SET DEFAULT.
